  pm-kfq5,related,"2026-11-07T13:26:16.419Z","harness:claude-code","cli:create:dep",detected
comments[4]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-07T17:52:53.178Z","harness:claude-code","Implemented on branch fix/cli-option-contract-truthfulness. Root cause: Commander registers both spellings of a declared extension flag as one option, so --status-filter open -s closed sets one attribute (last wins) and removes both tokens from command.args, and the loose occurrence parser never sees either spelling. Fix: captureCommandOptionTokens (src/cli/runtime/invocation-options.ts) patches each wrapped command's parseOptions to record the tokens Commander hands that command's own parser (the root has already consumed the host flags). refuseConflictingArgvOptionSpellings (src/sdk/extension/command-options.ts) scans those tokens with Commander's value rules (separate, --long=value, -sVALUE, optional values not read from a following option, switches with a following positional count as presence), using the command's own arity table so another option's value is never read as a flag. It refuses with extension_option_spelling_conflict when two spellings of one declared scalar flag hold different values after the declared coercion. Equal values (--count 1 -c 1.0), repeats of one spelling and list flags are unchanged.\n\nVerification: the built-CLI spec covers the conflict refusal, attached forms, equal values, list accumulation and repeated-spelling last-wins. The in-process spec includes a negative control: without the captured tokens Commander keeps \"closed\" silently. The SDK unit spec covers every scanner branch. Docs: SDK.md \"Handler keys and ownership\" and EXTENSIONS.md registerFlags.\n"
  "2026-11-07T18:21:52.356Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 (gpt-6.1-sol, xhigh, read-only) found five scan gaps here, all reproduced and fixed in commit c6210382d. (1) Short groups were skipped: --status-filter open -vs closed ran with closed, and -vt -s was falsely refused although Commander binds -s as the --tags value. (2) A separated negative number was not taken as an optional value (--count=-1 -c -1 was falsely refused). (3) A core option that owns a colliding short alias (close keeps -r for --reason) was read as the extension flag. (4) When parent and child both declare the flag, the parent parser consumes both spellings, so the child's own tokens are empty and the conflict was missed. (5) --verbose=false -v was missed, because Commander leaves --switch=value unparsed while the loose parser delivers it. The scanner is now a port of Commander's parseOptions loop over every command on the dispatch path. Ownership comes from the registered Commander options, and negative numbers are values unless the path has a numeric short option. --switch=<literal> and --no-<switch> count for a declared boolean. Each case has a unit spec, and the ancestor and ownership cases run on real Commander programs.\n"
  "2026-11-07T18:40:45.111Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 2 found three narrower scan gaps here, fixed in commit 8f4c6f6ba. (1) pm namespaces enable positional options, so a parent stops reading its own options at its subcommand. The ancestor scan read the whole argv and falsely refused pm item --message why close pm-one -r done, where close keeps -r for --reason. Each scope now applies Commander's stop rule (subcommand or alias before any other unrecognized token; first unrecognized token for pass-through), and only tokens Commander consumes count as recognized. (2) Declared flags Commander did not register are read by the loose parser, so the scan uses its grammar for them: -v=false attaches, no short groups, no dash-leading values. (3) --no-<flag> for a legacy optional bool declaration now counts. Each case has a unit spec, and the stop rules also run on real Commander programs with enablePositionalOptions and passThroughOptions.\n"
  "2026-11-07T18:57:01.812Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 3 found four more scan paths, fixed in commit 0ea4615fe: unparsed --switch=<literal> recorded in an ancestor scope (now leaf-only), loose switches taking a following token as parseLooseOptionToken does, variadic options consuming operands before the positional-options stop rule, and registered core --no-<flag> options on the declared boolean's attribute. Each has a unit spec."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-07T18:21:53.106Z","harness:claude-code","Commander lets every command on the dispatch path consume its own options anywhere after its name, and it folds all spellings of an option into one attribute (last wins). So no single command's args ever show both spellings, and an ancestor can swallow a leaf's flag. To audit what the caller typed, capture each command's parseOptions input and replay Commander's own rules (groups, required/optional values, negative numbers, --long=value) per scope."
